From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Varagnat <emmanuel.varagnat@vates.tech>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 10:49:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] libqcow2: fix use-after-free in qemu_deinit_main_loop()
 teardown order

AioContext is allocated as a GSource -- aio_context_new() does
g_source_new(&aio_source_funcs, sizeof(AioContext)), so the GSource and
the AioContext are one and the same allocation. The unref/destroy/unref
sequence in qemu_deinit_main_loop() can therefore drop the context's
refcount to zero and free the whole AioContext, including its bh_list.

Two cleanups then ran *after* that point on freed memory:

  - qemu_bh_delete(qemu_notify_bh), whose bottom half belongs to
    qemu_aio_context (created by qemu_bh_new() in qemu_init_main_loop());
    aio_bh_enqueue() dereferences bh->ctx.
  - the sigfd teardown, which calls aio_set_fd_handler() on
    iohandler_get_aio_context() after that context's GSource has been
    destroyed just above; qemu_lockcnt_lock() then touches freed memory.

Both are plain ordering bugs -- move them ahead of the corresponding
GSource teardown. The refcount arithmetic itself is fine and is left
alone: aio_get_g_source() takes an extra g_source_ref(), so the triple
unref/destroy/unref is balanced.

qemu_deinit_main_loop() has no caller anywhere in QEMU upstream (a normal
QEMU process never tears the main loop down, it just exit()s), so this
ordering had never been exercised. This fork is its only user, through
qcow2_free(), because it opens and closes a full QEMU main loop per image,
per worker thread.

Reproduced 100% of the time under ASan by a plain open-then-close cycle,
with no concurrency and no commit involved:

  ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free
    #0 aio_bh_enqueue           util/async.c
    #1 qemu_bh_delete           util/async.c
    #2 qemu_deinit_main_loop    util/main-loop.c
    #3 qcow2_free               drivers/block-qcow2.c

Caveat: the environment this was found in runs glib 2.88, while XCP-ng
dom0 runs glib 2.56. Without ASan, and with the worker thread exiting
right after, the corruption may well stay silent in production -- the code
is objectively wrong, but no real-world impact has been demonstrated.

Signed-off-by: Emmanuel Varagnat <emmanuel.varagnat@vates.tech>
---
 qcow2/lib/util/main-loop.c | 22 +++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/qcow2/lib/util/main-loop.c b/qcow2/lib/util/main-loop.c
index 8bde8add..9c77c73b 100644
--- a/qcow2/lib/util/main-loop.c
+++ b/qcow2/lib/util/main-loop.c
@@ -198,6 +198,19 @@ int qemu_deinit_main_loop(void)
 {
     GSource *src;
 
+    /*
+     * Delete qemu_notify_bh and sigfd first, while qemu_aio_context is still
+     * guaranteed alive.
+     */
+    qemu_bh_delete(qemu_notify_bh);
+    qemu_notify_bh = NULL;
+
+    if (sigfd != -1) {
+        aio_set_fd_handler(iohandler_get_aio_context(), sigfd, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
+        close(sigfd);
+        sigfd = -1;
+    }
+
     src = iohandler_get_g_source();
     g_source_unref(src);
     g_source_destroy(src);
@@ -210,15 +223,6 @@ int qemu_deinit_main_loop(void)
 
     g_array_free(gpollfds, TRUE);
 
-    qemu_bh_delete(qemu_notify_bh);
-    qemu_notify_bh = NULL;
-
-    if (sigfd != -1) {
-        aio_set_fd_handler(iohandler_get_aio_context(), sigfd, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
-        close(sigfd);
-        sigfd = -1;
-    }
-
     iohandler_deinit();
     qemu_aio_context = NULL;
 
